  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 AM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES water vapor imagery reveals a stout upper level \nridge parked over the central CONUS supporting a sprawling surface \nhigh pressure that extends up the Mississippi River Valley up to the \nwestern Great Lakes. This has return dry and quiet weather to the \nregion with clear skies across the UP/Lake Superior. These features \ndominate through the end of the weekend into early this week, \nmoderating temperatures well above normal as southerly return flow \nkicks in behind the southeastward progression of the surface high. \nForecast temps Tuesday-Thursday are expected to climb into the low \n50s, some 15-20 degrees above the climo normal for mid November in \nthe UP. The next shot at measurable precip (rain, not snow \nunfortunately) arrives late this week into early this weekend as \ntroughing in the western US / Rockies lifts into the Upper Midwest \nand Great Lakes. At this time, widespread meaningful rain chances \nlook bleak as the NBM highlights a 40-60% chance for amounts >0.01\" \n(the definition of measurable precipitation) across the UP by \nThursday night. \n\nDeterministic global models and their ensemble counterparts continue \nto highlight a period of potentially active weather (rain and snow) \nreturning next weekend into early next week, featuring a deep trough \nlifting a surface low through the Great Lakes area. Its still way \ntoo early to call specifics, as each suite show key differences in \nupper level and surface feature placement, timing, and strength, but \ninterested parties and pre-Thanksgiving travelers should remain in \ntune to the extended forecast should a colder, snowier trend result \nin impacts to travel and holiday festivities.",
